Deduplicate the matrix structure as it is built, instead of accumulating it.

mastruct.c builds the sparse structure by pushing ONE linked-list entry per
(dof,dof) pair of every element onto mast1/next -- growing them 1.1x through a
32-bit ITG index -- and compressing only afterwards, in the sort + "removing
duplicate entries" passes.  So the transient list is the SUM of the per-element
patterns while the structure that survives is their UNION.

For F-barES-FEM-T4 those differ by a large factor.  Measured on the LCOL4 sea
ice cell, undrained, c = 1:

  L_mesh    pairs offered   distinct (final nnz)   ratio
  0.0240      4.02e8              16 378 633       24.6x
  0.0120      1.66e9              68 318 770       24.3x

The same (dof,dof) pair is produced by ~24 different edges, because a U3
element's ring x support stencil overlaps its neighbours' heavily.  The
duplication is entirely ACROSS elements: within one element the jj/ll loop
already emits each pair exactly once, so a per-element "direct assembly" of
U3's known ring x support pattern cannot remove any of it.  (Counted on the
0.0120 deck: the loop makes 1.4394e9 U3 insertions, while sum(3*nring * 3*nope)
-- the size a direct builder would preallocate -- is 1.5626e9, LARGER.)

What the transient costs is what blocks the finer meshes.  At 0.0120 it is
11.7 GB and the run peaks at 22.3 GB; at 0.0080 it would be ~5.3e9 entries and
at 0.0060 ~1.3e10, both past the 2^31 = 2.147e9 an ITG can index, so ccx dies
in u_realloc with a negative allocation size.  INTSIZE64 raises that ceiling
but doubles the entry to 16 B, needing ~80 GB and ~190 GB -- neither machine
has it.  Removing the transient is the only route to 0.0060.

  mastructdedup.c   two backends that collapse duplicates as they arrive
  mastruct.c        routes insert()/insertfreq() through them and, when one
                    is armed, builds irow/jq straight from the sorted key set
  CalculiX.h        prototypes
  Makefile.inc      new source

IT IS OPT-IN.  CCX_MASTRUCT_DEDUP selects the backend:

  legacy (default)  the original linked list, untouched -- stock runs take the
                    same code path they always did
  chunk             a fixed buffer (CCX_MASTRUCT_BUF=<MB>, default 1024) is
                    radix-sorted, uniqued, and back-merged into one running
                    sorted run.  Peak memory is the buffer plus the final
                    structure, independent of how much duplication there is.
  hash              an open-addressing hash set over packed (col,row) keys,
                    sorted once at the end.  Peak memory tracks the final
                    structure.

The element loop and every SPC/MPC branch are untouched -- only the sink the
pairs flow into changes -- so the constrained-DOF expansion that the generic
path performs is performed identically here.  Substructures (mastructread) and
the frequency/modal boundary block still walk ipointer/next directly, so both
refuse to arm: nmethod 2/4/5-7 fall back to legacy with a message, and a
superelement is a hard error telling you to rerun with legacy.

VERIFIED ANSWER-NEUTRAL.  Every backend produces bit-identical structure and
bit-identical reactions on the acceptance decks (LCOL4, undrained, c = 1,
unsymmetric path, PARDISO out of core with a 16384 MB budget):

  L_mesh   backend   equations   nonzero lower triangular   F_x on RP-1
  0.0240   legacy      177 848            16 378 633        1.552992E+07
  0.0240   chunk       177 848            16 378 633        1.552992E+07
  0.0240   hash        177 848            16 378 633        1.552992E+07
  0.0120   legacy      603 467            68 318 770        1.208091E+07
  0.0120   chunk       603 467            68 318 770        1.208091E+07
  0.0120   hash        603 467            68 318 770        1.208091E+07

i.e. E_x = 6.212 GPa at 0.0240 and 4.832 GPa at 0.0120, the values the legacy
binary returns.  The remaining differences in the .dat files are the transverse
reactions on RP-2/RP-3, which are ~1e-14 of the driving reaction and differ by
the same amount between two runs of the SAME binary -- the threaded assembly is
not bitwise reproducible, and that predates this patch.

CHUNK IS THE ONE TO USE.  At 0.0120 both backends produce the same structure,
but chunk builds it in 26.2 s against hash's 58.4 s -- hash pays for 1.66e9
random probes into a table too big to cache.  Their peak footprints are equal
at this size (3.47 vs 3.46 GB), but they scale differently: chunk's is a
buffer you set (CCX_MASTRUCT_BUF) plus the run array, while hash's is the
table itself, which must be over-provisioned and briefly holds both halves
during a rehash.  chunk is what the finer meshes should run.

WHAT IT BUYS.  At 0.0120 the structure phase drops from an 11.7 GB transient to
3.47 GB peak and 26 s.  The total peak RSS does NOT improve there (22.7 GB
against 22.3 GB), because the out-of-core factorisation's core budget dominates
once the structure is built.  The point is not 0.0120 -- it is that at 0.0080
and 0.0060 the transient is 4.6e9 and ~1.3e10 entries, past what a 32-bit ITG
can index, so the legacy path cannot build the structure at ALL, at any amount
of RAM.  Those two meshes are the reason this exists.

